<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">French conquest of Corsica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1769">1769</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1768, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> ceded its rights over the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Corsican Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a> resisted, but was defeated at Ponte Novu in May 1769, and Paoli went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Corsica became a province of France, a few months before the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> in Ajaccio.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-conquest-of-corsica.jpg" alt="French conquest of Corsica" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Ponte_Novu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> since 1284. In 1729 the Corsican Revolution for independence began, led first by Luiggi Giafferi and Giacinto Paoli and later by Giacinto's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a>. After a short-lived attempt in 1736 to proclaim a Kingdom of Corsica under the German adventurer Theodor von Neuhoff, the independent Corsican Republic was proclaimed in 1755. Paoli wrote its constitution in Italian, the language of culture on the island, but the republic never managed to drive the Genoese from the coastal fortresses of Calvi and Bonifacio.</p><p>After the Corsicans took Capraia, a small island of the Tuscan Archipelago, in 1767, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, exhausted by decades of fighting, decided to sell the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which after its defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> was trying to strengthen its position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> sent an expedition in May 1768 to secure French rule. The first, rather small expeditionary force marched inland and suffered an unexpected and significant defeat at the Battle of Borgo in October 1768. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> then sent large reinforcements, raising its army on the island to 24,000 men, and under the Comte de Vaux, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, it decisively defeated the Corsican army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ponte-novu">Battle of Ponte Novu</a>. Many Corsicans took to the hills and fought on as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, so that consolidating French control took about a year, with sporadic rebellions after that.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The invasion set off the Corsican Crisis in British politics: the British government sent secret aid to the Corsicans but chose not to prevent the occupation. Paoli lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where he was very popular and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson">Samuel Johnson</a>'s dining club, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> allowed him to return. In 1794 an Anglo-Corsican expedition took the island and set up the Anglo-Corsican Kingdom, but the French reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> on 19 October 1796, and it became a French département. Italian remained the official language until 1859.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, born on the island in 1769, was a French citizen from birth, although his parents, Carlo Maria Buonaparte and Maria Letizia Ramolino, had fought in the resistance against the French while Maria was pregnant with him. Raised as a Corsican nationalist, he gradually turned his loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and went on to rule it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French conquest of Corsica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
